nce Profiler is probably your best bet.\n\n# C# & ASP.Net - determine linq query generation time\n\nAsk Question\n\nAsked 15 years, 10 months ago\n\nModified 15 years, 10 months ago\n\nViewed 185 times\n\n\nI'd like to detemine the amount of time it takes for my ASP.Net program to generate certain sql queries using linq.... note - I want the query generation time, not the query execution time. Is this possible, or even feasable (if its usually fast)?\n\nMy website has some heavy traffic and I want to cover all of my bases.\n\n  * c#\n  * linq\n  * linq-to-entities\n\n\n## 2 Answers 2\n\nSorted by:\n\n[Select]\n\n[Input]\n\n1\n\n[Input]\n\nRedgate ANTS Performance Profiler is probably your best bet.\n\nImprove this answer\n\nFollow [Input]\n\nanswered Jun 18, 2010 at 15:46\n\nAren\n\n56.1k 9 9 gold badges 73 73 silver badges 101 101 bronze badges\n\n\n1\n\n[Input]\n\nStopwatch + CompiledQuery.Compile ?\n\nImprove this answer\n\nFollow [Input]\n\nanswered Jun 18, 2010 at 15:49\n\nDenis Palnitsky\n\n18.4k 14 14 gold badges 50 50 silver badges 57 57 bronze badges--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nIn Russia, they came up with a neuroprocessor that can push NVidia - ВПК.name (https://vpk.name/en/461475_in-russia-they-came-up-with-a-neuroprocessor-that-can-push-nvidia.html)\nciteturn181search10 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.0 years ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; The Russian company IVA Technologies, part of the high-Tech group, has completed the development of a new completely original microprocessor architecture, and it has demonstrated impressive results in international tests. ... \"The analysis of the results shows that for one of the most common neural networks (Resnet-50), the IVA TPU architecture provides three times less latency than the Xilinx Alveo U250. ... \"Successful participation in testing using a generally accepted method with a strict audit of the results is primarily an indicator of the maturity of the solution, its readiness for industrial use,\" said IVA Technologies Deputy CEO, head of the Department, in a conversation with CNews Evgeny Terentyev. ... In April 2020 CNews found out that within three years, Hightech will create and launch its own enterprise-level software and hardware communication platform-a competitor to Cisco and Avaya, spending a little more than 1 billion rubles.\n\n10.11.2020\n\nВремя электроники\n\n# In Russia, they came up with a neuroprocessor that can push NVidia\n\nSections: General industry information, Electronics and optics, Markets and cooperation, New development\n\nThe Russian company IVA Technologies, part of the high-Tech group, has completed the development of a new completely original microprocessor architecture, and it has demonstrated impressive results in international tests. The appearance of chips based on it is expected in early 2021.\n\nAs CNews found out, in Russia in the first quarter of 2021, the first samples of domestic chips implemented on the latest IVA TPU processor architecture will be released. It was developed by the Russian company IVA Technologies, which is part of the high-Tech group.\n\nThe promise of this neural network-oriented architecture was confirmed during testing at the MLPerf site in late October 2020. This organization is an international consortium that was established in 2018 to develop universal tests that measure the effectiveness of artificial intelligence systems. CNews) the results will be better than our closest competitor NVidia Jetson Xavier NX-despite the lag in technology: 28 nm vs. 12 nm.\"\n\n\"Successful participation in testing using a generally accepted method with a strict audit of the results is primarily an indicator of the maturity of the solution, its readiness for industrial use,\" said IVA Technologies Deputy CEO, head of the Department, in a conversation with CNews Evgeny Terentyev.\n\nAccording to him, most of the neuroprocessors in the world, positioned as independent solutions, are actually a compilation of cores with third-party architecture. IVA Technologies has a completely original architecture. Terentyev has no doubt that it will be in demand not only in Russia, but also abroad.\n\nAccording to IVA, the above-mentioned Closed Division Edge group, which tested the architecture, has only four vendors in the world so far: Nvidia, ARM, Centaur, and Xilinx. IVA Technologies was the fifth.\n\n## There will be no competition with \"Elbrus \" and\" Baikal\"\n\nThe development of the IVA TPU architecture, as Terentyev notes, began in the company at the beginning of 2016, and then this project caused complete bewilderment of colleagues in the market. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEntity Framework Performance Resources – manuelmeyer.net (https://manuelmeyer.net/2016/01/entity-framework-performance-resources/)\nciteturn181search11 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10.8 years ago; Crawled: 4 weeks ago; Part 1, provides an index-style summary of two great articles on EF performance optimizations and part 2 introduces two lightweight tools that I often use when looking at EF performance issues, ExpressProfiler and LINQPad.\n\n## Part 2: Tooling\n\nWhile I am talking about EF, I would like to mention some tools that can be very helpful when analyzing EF performance. The obvious tools are a .NET performance profiler and/or a profiler for Sql Server queries.\n\n### Express Profiler\n\nVery often, you are facing a challenging performance problem related to Sql Server query performance and you are missing the tooling. Either because you are not allowed to install anything on the machine or you don’t have a profiling tool available. If you are interested in the SQL queries that are sent to the server, ExpressProfiler comes to the rescue. It is a super lightweight application that lets you record the sql statements sent to an Sql Server database. And by super lightweight, I mean SUPER lightweight. It is as small as around 150 kilobytes and does not need any installation. Go get it now, it’s free and it’s great!\n\nhttps://expressprofiler.codeplex.com\n\n \n\n### LINQPad\n\nLINQPad (http://www.linqpad.net/) is another great tool that I use very often. It is a scratch pad for LINQ statements. Start it, connect to a DB, write a LINQ statement and immediately get the results. You cannot be any faster than this. It is lightweight (8MB), fast and the trial is free. However, I recommend to get the full version in order to have complete Visual Studio like intellisense. Paid versions are between USD 45 and USD 85 for a single user.\n\nI often use LINQPad to fiddle around until I find the perfect LINQ statement to solve my problem, before I add it to my Visual Studio Project. LINQPad will show you the results, the execution time and the entire SQL statement."
